<!-- 5. ABSTRACT + KEYWORDS -->
<h1 class="center">ABSTRACT</h1>
<p>The manual diagnosis of brain tumors using Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a time-consuming and highly subjective process that requires specialized radiological expertise. The advent of Deep Learning, particularly Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), offers a robust avenue for automating this diagnostic workflow. This project presents a full-stack, cloud-deployed Brain Tumor MRI Classification and Localization System capable of categorizing MRI scans into four distinct classes: Glioma, Meningioma, Pituitary Tumor, and No Tumor. An EfficientNet-B3 transfer learning architecture was fine-tuned for high-accuracy feature extraction. To bridge the gap between black-box AI and clinical transparency, the system integrates Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM), which visually highlights the specific regions of the MRI influencing the model's prediction. The system is designed with a strong focus on scalable DevOps methodologies; it is containerized and deployed on Amazon Web Services (AWS EC2) via an automated GitHub deployment pipeline and rendered through an interactive Streamlit frontend. Additionally, Apache PySpark was integrated into the data pipeline to handle scalable metadata processing, demonstrating the system's readiness for large-scale hospital datasets. This project successfully combines Machine Learning, Cloud Computing, and DevOps into a cohesive, highly accessible medical imaging platform.</p>
<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> <em>Brain Tumor Classification, MRI, EfficientNet, Grad-CAM, AWS EC2, Streamlit, PySpark, DevOps, Explainable AI.</em></p>

