<!-- 7. INTRODUCTION -->
<h1>1. INTRODUCTION</h1>
<p>The early and accurate detection of brain tumors is a critical factor in improving patient survival rates and determining appropriate therapeutic interventions. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) remains the gold standard for non-invasive brain tumor analysis due to its superior soft-tissue contrast. However, interpreting thousands of MRI slices is an exhaustive manual task for radiologists, often leading to delayed diagnoses and inter-observer variability.</p>
<p>In recent years, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has significantly transformed healthcare, particularly in the domain of medical image analysis. Deep learning models can rapidly analyze complex medical images, identify anomalies, and assist clinical professionals as secondary diagnostic tools. Despite these advancements, standard deep learning models act as "black boxes," providing predictions without explaining the underlying rationale. This lack of transparency limits the clinical adoption of AI.</p>
<p>To address this, this project develops an end-to-end Brain Tumor Classification System that not only predicts the tumor type but also utilizes Explainable AI (Grad-CAM) to generate a visual localization heatmap. Furthermore, an AI model's real-world utility is heavily dependent on its accessibility. Thus, the system is fully integrated into a modern DevOps pipeline and deployed on an AWS EC2 cloud infrastructure, ensuring 24/7 global accessibility and scalable data handling via PySpark.</p>

<!-- 8. LITERATURE REVIEW -->
<h1>2. LITERATURE REVIEW</h1>
<p>The application of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) in medical imaging has been extensively researched. Initial studies relied on custom CNN architectures, which often suffered from overfitting on limited medical datasets. The paradigm subsequently shifted toward Transfer Learning, where models pretrained on vast datasets (like ImageNet) are fine-tuned for specific medical tasks.</p>
<p>Recent studies highlight the efficacy of the EfficientNet family of models. EfficientNet optimizes the depth, width, and resolution of networks uniformly, proving to be highly effective for high-resolution medical image classification while minimizing computational overhead compared to traditional ResNet architectures.</p>
<p>On the front of model transparency, Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) has emerged as the standard for visual explanations. By utilizing the gradients of the target concept flowing into the final convolutional layer, Grad-CAM produces a coarse localization map that highlights the important regions in the image. </p>
<p>Finally, the transition of ML models from local notebooks to cloud environments has gained traction. Cloud infrastructure (like AWS) combined with web frameworks (like Streamlit) and Big Data tools (like Apache PySpark) enables the deployment of scalable, distributed healthcare architectures.</p>

<!-- 9. RESEARCH GAPS -->
<h1>3. RESEARCH GAPS</h1>
<p>Based on the literature review, the following research gaps were identified in existing automated MRI diagnostic systems:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Lack of Transparency:</strong> Many existing models output high-accuracy predictions but fail to explain their logic, preventing clinical trust (the "black-box" problem).</li>
    <li><strong>Poor Deployment Strategies:</strong> The majority of academic AI models remain confined to local Jupyter Notebooks and lack real-world cloud deployment architectures.</li>
    <li><strong>Inadequate Scalability:</strong> Existing pipelines often process data locally in memory, making them incapable of handling large-scale, enterprise-level hospital databases spanning thousands of gigabytes.</li>
</ul>

<!-- 10. PROBLEM DEFINITION -->
<h1>4. PROBLEM DEFINITION</h1>
<p>The primary problem addressed in this project is the lack of a transparent, scalable, and globally accessible AI-assisted diagnostic tool for brain tumors. The goal is to build a system capable of accurately classifying MRI scans into four categories (Glioma, Meningioma, Pituitary, No Tumor) while providing visual proof (Grad-CAM) of the diagnosis. The system must overcome traditional local-hardware limitations by leveraging a cloud-based AWS infrastructure, adopting CI/CD DevOps practices, and integrating Big Data frameworks (PySpark) to future-proof the data processing pipeline.</p>

<!-- 11. OBJECTIVES -->
<h1>5. OBJECTIVES</h1>
<p>The core objectives of this project are as follows:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>MRI Classification:</strong> To fine-tune an EfficientNet-B3 deep learning model to accurately classify brain MRI scans into four distinct tumor categories.</li>
    <li><strong>Explainable AI Implementation:</strong> To integrate Grad-CAM visualizations that highlight the specific MRI regions influencing the model's prediction, thereby eliminating the black-box effect.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud Deployment:</strong> To establish a robust DevOps pipeline and deploy the application on an AWS EC2 instance for uninterrupted, cross-platform accessibility.</li>
    <li><strong>Big Data Scalability:</strong> To implement an Apache PySpark data pipeline capable of handling large-scale dataset analytics and metadata processing for future scalability.</li>
</ul>

<!-- 12. METHODOLOGY -->
<h1>6. METHODOLOGY</h1>
<p>The methodology follows a structured pipeline from data preprocessing to cloud deployment.</p>
<h2>6.1 Workflow Architecture</h2>
<p>The system workflow executes in the following sequence:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>MRI Upload:</strong> The user uploads a standard MRI scan (JPG/PNG) via the Streamlit dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Preprocessing:</strong> The image is resized to 300x300 pixels, and array normalization is applied.</li>
    <li><strong>EfficientNet Model:</strong> The preprocessed image is passed through the pre-trained EfficientNet-B3 model for feature extraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Classification & Confidence:</strong> The model's dense layers apply a softmax function to generate confidence probability scores for each of the four classes.</li>
    <li><strong>Grad-CAM Visualization:</strong> Concurrently, a GradientTape records the operations during the forward pass. Gradients from the <code>top_conv</code> layer are extracted, pooled, and power-normalized to generate a heatmap overlaid on the original image.</li>
    <li><strong>AWS Cloud Output:</strong> The results are formatted using interactive Plotly charts and rendered dynamically to the user via the AWS-hosted Streamlit frontend.</li>
</ol>
<br>
