<!-- 15. IMPLEMENTATION & AWS DEPLOYMENT -->
<h1>9. IMPLEMENTATION & AWS DEPLOYMENT</h1>
<p>The deployment strategy bridges Machine Learning and DevOps, migrating the application from local development to a highly available AWS EC2 environment.</p>

<h2>9.1 GitHub Integration</h2>
<p>The entire source code, including Python scripts, requirements files, and the trained Keras model, is hosted on a secure GitHub repository. This acts as the single source of truth for the CI/CD deployment pipeline, enabling rapid rollbacks and version control.</p>

<h2>9.2 AWS EC2 Provisioning & Environment Setup</h2>
<p>An AWS EC2 <code>t3.medium</code> instance was provisioned running Amazon Linux 2023. To ensure stable public access, an AWS Elastic IP was allocated and associated with the instance. Security groups were strictly configured to allow incoming traffic on SSH (Port 22), HTTP/HTTPS (Ports 80/443), and the specific Streamlit application port (Port 8501).</p>
<p>The deployment followed these terminal procedures via SSH:</p>
<ol>
    <li>System update and installation of Python 3 and Git using the <code>dnf</code> package manager.</li>
    <li>Cloning the project repository from GitHub.</li>
    <li>Creation of an isolated Python Virtual Environment (<code>venv</code>) to prevent dependency conflicts.</li>
    <li>Installation of strict dependency versions (e.g., <code>numpy<2</code>, <code>tensorflow==2.16.1</code>) via <code>pip</code>.</li>
    <li>Installation of OpenJDK to satisfy the JVM requirements for the PySpark pipeline.</li>
</ol>

<h2>9.3 Application Execution</h2>
<p>To ensure the Streamlit web server remains active 24/7, even after the SSH session is terminated, the application was launched as a background daemon process using the <code>nohup</code> command:</p>
<p><code>nohup streamlit run app.py --server.port 8501 --server.address 0.0.0.0 &</code></p>
<br>
